```

You should see:

PartitionCount: 3

ReplicationFactor: 1


Lab 2 – Create a Producer and Publish Records

Duration: 10 Minutes

We will use a keyed producer so students can observe which partitions receive the records.

```bash
sudo docker exec -it kafka-day1 \
  /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-console-producer.sh \
  --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
  --topic day4-orders \
  --property parse.key=true \
  --property key.separator=:
```

Enter these records:

```text
ORDER101:Order Created
ORDER102:Order Created
ORDER103:Order Created
ORDER101:Payment Completed
ORDER102:Payment Completed
ORDER103:Payment Completed
ORDER101:Order Shipped
ORDER102:Order Shipped
ORDER103:Order Shipped
```

Press Enter after every record.

Keep this terminal running.


Lab 3 – Start Your First Consumer

Duration: 10 Minutes

Open a second EC2 terminal.

Run:

```bash
sudo docker exec -it kafka-day1 \
  /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-console-consumer.sh \
  --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
  --topic day4-orders \
  --group order-group \
  --from-beginning \
  --property print.key=true \
  --property print.partition=true \
  --property print.offset=true
```

Expected Behavior:

- Existing messages are displayed.
- Each record shows its key, partition, and offset.
- Events for the same order key should be in the same partition under the standard keyed partitioner.
- Records from different partitions may appear in different relative order.

Explanation:

--from-beginning starts at the earliest available offset when the group has no valid committed position.

It does not override an existing valid committed offset.


Lab 4 – Run Multiple Consumers in the Same Group

Duration: 15 Minutes

Keep Consumer 1 running.

Terminal 3 – Consumer 2

```bash
sudo docker exec -it kafka-day1 \
  /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-console-consumer.sh \
  --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
  --topic day4-orders \
  --group order-group \
  --property print.partition=true
```

Terminal 4 – Consumer 3

```bash
sudo docker exec -it kafka-day1 \
  /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-console-consumer.sh \
  --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
  --topic day4-orders \
  --group order-group \
  --property print.partition=true
```

All three consumers belong to the same group.

Inspect Partition Assignment

In another terminal:

```bash
sudo docker exec kafka-day1 \
  /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-consumer-groups.sh \
  --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
  --describe \
  --group order-group \
  --members \
  --verbose
```

Expected:

Three active members and their partition assignments after the group stabilizes.

Each consumer should have one partition assigned in a balanced three-partition, three-consumer setup.

Publish More Records

Return to the producer terminal and enter:

```text
ORDER101:Delivered
ORDER102:Delivered
ORDER103:Delivered
```

Observe which consumer displays each event.

Trainer Question:

Why doesn't every consumer receive all three records?

Answer:

Because the consumers belong to the same group and divide partition ownership.

If they used separate group IDs, each group could independently read all retained records.


Lab 5 – Consumer Offsets and Lag

Duration: 15 Minutes

Step 1: Describe Consumer Group

```bash
sudo docker exec kafka-day1 \
  /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-consumer-groups.sh \
  --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
  --describe \
  --group order-group
```

Illustrative Output:

```text
GROUP       TOPIC        PARTITION CURRENT-OFFSET LOG-END-OFFSET LAG
order-group day4-orders  0         4              4              0
order-group day4-orders  1         4              4              0
order-group day4-orders  2         4              4              0
```

The actual offsets and distribution depend on how records were partitioned and when commits occurred.

Step 2: Understand Consumer Lag

Formula:

Lag = Log-end offset - Committed offset

Example:

Log-end offset: 100

Committed offset: 75

Consumer lag: 25

This means the group's committed position is 25 offsets behind the log end.

Step 3: Demonstrate Lag

Stop all three consumers using Ctrl+C.

Wait briefly for their last auto-commit and group shutdown.

Now publish 20 messages:

```bash
seq 1 20 | sed 's/^/Pending-Order-/' | \
sudo docker exec -i kafka-day1 \
  /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-console-producer.sh \
  --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
  --topic day4-orders
```

Check the Group Again:

```bash
sudo docker exec kafka-day1 \
  /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-consumer-groups.sh \
  --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
  --describe \
  --group order-group
```

Expected:

Lag should increase if the group's committed offsets remain behind the newly appended records.

Restart a Consumer:

```bash
sudo docker exec -it kafka-day1 \
  /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-console-consumer.sh \
  --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
  --topic day4-orders \
  --group order-group
```

Watch the pending messages get consumed.

Run the group-description command again after a few seconds.

The lag should decrease as the consumer processes and commits offsets.

Note:

Console consumers auto-commit periodically.

Reported lag is based on committed positions and may not update immediately.


Lab 6 – Consumer Failure and Rebalancing

Duration: 10 Minutes

Start three consumers in order-group again.

Use:

```bash
sudo docker exec kafka-day1 \
  /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-consumer-groups.sh \
  --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
  --describe \
  --group order-group \
  --members \
  --verbose
```

Record their assignments.

Now stop one consumer with Ctrl+C.

Wait for the group to stabilize, then rerun the command.

Expected Behavior:

- The stopped consumer leaves the group.
- Its partitions are reassigned to remaining members.
- The remaining consumers continue processing.

Explanation:

Graceful shutdown and unexpected failure can have different detection times.

Rebalancing behavior also depends on the configured group protocol.

