LAB 3 – DEMONSTRATE RETENTION AND SEGMENT CLEANUP

Duration: 20 minutes

For a classroom demonstration, we can use a short retention interval and a small segment size.

Step 1: Create a separate demo topic

```bash
sudo docker exec kafka-day1 \
  /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-topics.sh \
  --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
  --create \
  --if-not-exists \
  --topic day5-cleanup-demo \
  --partitions 1 \
  --replication-factor 1 \
  --config retention.ms=30000 \
  --config segment.bytes=1048576 \
  --config segment.ms=10000 \
  --config cleanup.policy=delete
```

This configures:

| Configuration | Value |
|---|---|
| Retention | 30 seconds |
| Segment target size | 1 MiB |
| Segment roll interval | 10 seconds |
| Cleanup policy | Delete |

Step 2: Publish 100 records

```bash
seq 1 100 | \
sudo docker exec -i kafka-day1 \
  /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-console-producer.sh \
  --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
  --topic day5-cleanup-demo
```

Step 3: Check latest offsets

```bash
sudo docker exec kafka-day1 \
  /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-get-offsets.sh \
  --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
  --topic day5-cleanup-demo \
  --time -1
```

This reports the latest offset position.

Step 4: Check earliest offsets

```bash
sudo docker exec kafka-day1 \
  /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-get-offsets.sh \
  --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
  --topic day5-cleanup-demo \
  --time -2
```

Step 5: Observe retention cleanup

After enough time for segment rolling and cleanup, repeat the earliest-offset command.

Expected observation:

The earliest available offset may advance as Kafka deletes expired segments.

The exact time is not guaranteed. The broker's retention-check interval and segment eligibility affect when cleanup occurs.

If nothing changes immediately, do not treat it as a failure.

Retention is asynchronous, and active segments are not deleted merely because their records are old.

