PART 3 – DELIVERY SEMANTICS PRACTICAL

LAB 6 – DEMONSTRATE AT-LEAST-ONCE PROCESSING

Duration: 20 minutes

We will demonstrate why duplicate processing can occur when a consumer crashes before committing an offset.

Step 1: Create a new topic

```bash
sudo docker exec kafka-day1 \
  /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-topics.sh \
  --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
  --create \
  --if-not-exists \
  --topic day5-delivery \
  --partitions 1 \
  --replication-factor 1
```

Step 2: Publish a payment event

```bash
printf 'TXN101:Payment Completed\n' | \
sudo docker exec -i kafka-day1 \
  /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-console-producer.sh \
  --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
  --topic day5-delivery \
  --property parse.key=true \
  --property key.separator=:
```

Step 3: Read without committing

```bash
sudo docker exec kafka-day1 \
  /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-console-consumer.sh \
  --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
  --topic day5-delivery \
  --group delivery-demo-group \
  --from-beginning \
  --consumer-property enable.auto.commit=false \
  --max-messages 1
```

Expected output:

```text
Payment Completed
```

Step 4: Run the same consumer again

```bash
sudo docker exec kafka-day1 \
  /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-console-consumer.sh \
  --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 \
  --topic day5-delivery \
  --group delivery-demo-group \
  --from-beginning \
  --consumer-property enable.auto.commit=false \
  --max-messages 1
```

Expected output:

```text
Payment Completed
```

The same event appears again because this group has not committed its progress.

Trainer Explanation

This demonstrates how replay and duplicate processing can happen.

A real at-least-once application:
1. Reads a record.
2. Processes the record.
3. Commits the offset only after successful processing.

If it crashes between processing and committing, the record may be processed again.

--------------------------------------------------

7. DIFFERENCE BETWEEN KAFKA RETENTION AND OFFSET COMMITS

This is an important interview question.

| Retention | Offset Commit |
|---|---|
| Controls how long records remain available | Tracks consumer group progress |
| Configured on topic/broker | Managed by consumers |
| Can delete old records | Does not delete topic records |
| Applies independently of consumption | Helps consumers resume processing |

Example:

Even if a consumer has committed offset 100, Kafka may retain the first 100 records until retention cleanup makes them eligible for deletion.

--------------------------------------------------

